{pid:{type:<|\"|>INTEGER<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>pid<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_navigate{description:<|\"|>Open a URL in a persistent headless-browser session. The session keeps its state (page, JavaScript state, prior clicks and typed text) across all browser_* calls in this run. Returns the HTTP status, page title, page structure, and any console errors.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{url:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>url<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_click{description:<|\"|>Click an element in the browser session's current page. selector is a CSS selector (e.g. '#add-btn', 'button.save') or a text selector (e.g. 'text=Add task'). Returns the updated page structure and any console errors.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{selector:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>selector<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_type{description:<|\"|>Clear the input/textarea matched by selector in the browser session's current page and type text into it. Set press_enter to true to press Enter afterwards (e.g. to submit). Returns the updated page structure.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{press_enter:{type:<|\"|>BOOLEAN<|\"|>},selector:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},text:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>selector<|\"|>,<|\"|>text<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_drag{description:<|\"|>Drag the element matched by source onto the element matched by target in the browser session's current page (e.g. moving a card between columns). Both are CSS or text selectors. Works with HTML5 draggable elements and with mouse-based drag libraries. Returns the updated page structure, reports whether the page actually changed, and when it did not, which drag events the page received.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{source:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},target:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>source<|\"|>,<|\"|>target<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_read{description:<|\"|>Return the browser session's current page structure (interactive elements and text) plus any new console errors, without performing an action. With a selector, returns just that element's content.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{selector:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_wait{description:<|\"|>Wait up to ms milliseconds (default 1000, max 15000), or until the element matched by selector appears, then return the page state. Useful after starting a server or triggering a slow update.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{ms:{type:<|\"|>INTEGER<|\"|>},selector:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_new_page{description:<|\"|>Open an ADDITIONAL page in the same browser session and make it the active page, so a later browser_switch_page can come back to the earlier one. Pages behave like tabs of one browser: they share cookies and local storage, but each holds its own DOM, its own JavaScript state and its own open network connections. Optionally navigate the new page to url immediately. All other browser_* tools always act on the active page.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{url:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_switch_page{description:<|\"|>Make an already-open page the active one, so subsequent browser_* calls act on it. page is a label such as 'page-1' (the labels are listed in the OPEN PAGES line of any browser result once more than one page is open). Returns that page's current structure.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{page:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>page<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:browser_close_page{description:<|\"|>Close an open page, given its label (e.g. 'page-2'); with no argument, closes the active page. Another open page becomes active if there is one.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{page:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><turn|>\n<|turn>user\n## Context\n\nWe are building a traditional client-server web application with real-time capabilities. To minimize external dependencies while retaining the power of PostgreSQL, we are using PGLite embedded within a Node.js server. Real-time updates will be pushed to the client using Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- Provide a simple message board where users can post text messages.\n- Ensure all connected clients receive updates instantly when new messages are posted.\n- Store data persistently on the local disk using PGLite.\n- Keep the frontend incredibly simple and lightweight.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n- User authentication and authorization.\n- Rich text formatting or media attachments.\n- Horizontal scaling across multiple server instances (PGLite is tied to the single Node instance).\n\n## Decisions\n\n**Decision 1: Embedded PGLite vs External Postgres**\n- *Rationale*: PGLite allows us to run PostgreSQL directly inside the Node.js process. This means users don't need to install or configure a separate database service. It seamlessly handles raw SQL queries and can persist to the filesystem.\n\n**Decision 2: Server-Sent Events (SSE) vs WebSockets**\n- *Rationale*: We only need one-way real-time communication (Server pushing new messages to Clients). Clients can post new messages via standard HTTP POST requests. SSE is much simpler to implement, works natively over standard HTTP, and doesn't require a complex WebSocket library like Socket.io.\n\n**Decision 3: Vanilla JS / Vite vs React**\n- *Rationale*: Given the minimal UI requirements (a message list and a text input), Vanilla JS keeps the build extremely fast and the dependency tree small. Vite provides a great developer experience out of the box.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- [Risk] PGLite performance on very large datasets: Embedded WASM databases are incredibly fast but might struggle if the dataset grows to tens of gigabytes.\n  - *Mitigation*: This is a local application and we expect message volume to be manageable for a single server instance.\n- [Risk] SSE connection limits: Browsers have limits on the number of concurrent SSE connections per domain.\n  - *Mitigation*: We only need one SSE connection per client tab, which is well within browser limits.\n\n## Why\n\nWe need a fast, local-first collaborative message board that allows users to post messages and see updates in real-time. This provides an incredibly low-latency experience by keeping data local while ensuring all users see the same state instantly. It serves as an excellent demonstration of using embedded PGLite combined with Server-Sent Events (SSE) to create a robust, lightweight realtime application without external database dependencies.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- We are introducing a brand new traditional client-server web application.\n- The backend will be a Node.js server using an embedded PGLite database for raw SQL queries, persisting data to the local disk.\n- Real-time synchronization will be handled via Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n- The frontend will be a simple, lightweight Single Page Application (Vanilla JS or React via Vite).\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n- `realtime-board`: The core message board capability covering posting messages, viewing a message feed, and receiving live updates via SSE.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\nNone (Greenfield project).\n\n## Impact\n\nThis is a brand new project. It will establish the foundational architecture including the Node.js backend setup, the embedded PGLite database structure, the SSE connection handling, and the frontend web UI.\n\n## 1. Project Initialization\n\n- [ ] 1.1 Initialize the Node.js project and install backend dependencies (express, @electric-sql/pglite, cors)\n- [ ] 1.2 Initialize the Vanilla JS / Vite project for the frontend and install frontend dependencies\n- [ ] 1.3 Set up basic npm scripts for concurrently running backend and frontend development servers\n- [ ] 1.4 Make the app start from a fresh checkout with no manual steps: once the dependencies declared in its package.json files are installed, `npm run dev` at the project root starts both the backend and the frontend, the backend creates any directory it needs (such as PGLite's data directory), and the page the frontend serves reaches the backend's API and event stream\n\n## 2. Backend Infrastructure & Database\n\n- [ ] 2.1 Set up a basic Express server with CORS and JSON body parsing\n- [ ] 2.2 Initialize embedded PGLite configured to write to the local file system\n- [ ] 2.3 Create the database initialization script (create `messages` table with `id`, `text`, `created_at`)\n- [ ] 2.4 Create the historical messages endpoint (`GET /api/messages`) to fetch initial state\n\n## 3. Real-time Capabilities (SSE)\n\n- [ ] 3.1 Implement the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) that maintains active connections with clients\n- [ ] 3.2 Implement the messaging endpoint (`POST /api/messages`) to insert text into PGLite\n- [ ] 3.3 Broadcast new messages to all active SSE connections when a new message is successfully inserted\n\n## 4. Frontend Implementation\n\n- [ ] 4.1 Create the basic HTML layout containing a message list and a text input form\n- [ ] 4.2 Fetch the message history (`GET /api/messages`) on initial page load and render messages\n- [ ] 4.3 Connect to the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) using `EventSource` to listen for new messages\n- [ ] 4.4 Append incoming real-time messages to the DOM seamlessly\n- [ ] 4.5 Wire up the form submission to send a `POST` request to `/api/messages` and clear the input<turn|>\n<|turn>model\n<|channel>thought\nThe user wants me to implement a real-time message board using Node.js, PGLite, and SSE.\n\nHere's a plan:\n1.  **Project Initialization**:\n    *   Create a root `package.json` to manage both frontend and backend.\n    *   Create a `backend` directory with its own `package.json` and dependencies (`express`, `@electric-sql/pglite`, `cors`).\n    *   Create a `frontend` directory using Vite (Vanilla JS).\n    *   Set up a root `package.json` script to run both using `concurrently`.\n\n2.  **Backend Implementation**:\n    *   Create `backend/server.js`.\n    *   Initialize PGLite with a local directory for persistence.\n    *   Create the `messages` table.\n    *   Implement `GET /api/messages` to fetch history.\n    *   Implement `GET /api/stream` for SSE.\n    *   Implement `POST /api/messages` to save messages and broadcast them via SSE.\n\n3.  **Frontend Implementation**:\n    *   Create `frontend/index.html`.\n    *   Create `frontend/main.js` to handle fetching history, connecting to SSE, and posting messages.\n    *   Use simple CSS for basic styling.\n\n4.  **Testing**:\n    *   Run the application.\n    *   Verify that messages are persisted.\n    *   Verify that messages are broadcasted in real-time to multiple clients.\n\nLet's start with project initialization.\n<channel|><|tool_call>call:list_dir{path:<|\"|>.<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:list_dir{value:<|\"|>(empty)<|\"|>}<tool_response|>I will start by initializing the project structure. I'll create a root directory with a `package.json` that will manage both the backend and the frontend.\n\nFirst, I'll list the current directory to see where I am.<|channel>thought\nWait, I just did `list_dir` and it's empty. I'll start creating the files.\n\nRoot `package.json` for orchestrating.\nBackend folder.\nFrontend folder.\n\nI'll start by creating the backend and frontend directories and their respective `package.json` files.\n\nActually, I'll start by creating the root `package.json` and then the subdirectories.\n<channel|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"realtime-message-board\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"install-all\": \"npm install && npm install --prefix backend && npm install --prefix frontend\",\n    \"dev\": \"concurrently \\\"npm run dev --prefix backend\\\" \\\"npm run dev --prefix frontend\\\"\",\n    \"backend\": \"npm run dev --prefix backend\",\n    \"frontend\": \"npm run dev --prefix frontend\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"concurrently\": \"^8.2.2\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 445 chars to package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|channel>thought\nNow I'll create the backend.\n\n<channel|><|tool_call>call:run_command{command:<|\"|>mkdir backend<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:run_command{value:<|\"|>$ mkdir backend\n[exit 0]\n<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|channel>thought\nCreating backend `package.json`.\n\n<channel|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"backend\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"main\": \"server.js\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"node server.js\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@electric-sql/pglite\": \"^0.2.0\",\n    \"cors\": \"^2.8.5\",\n    \"express\": \"^4.18.2\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>backend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 227 chars to backend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|channel>thought\nNow I'll create the frontend using Vite. I'll do it manually by creating the necessary files instead of running a vite init command to be more precise.\n\n\n<channel|><|tool_call>call:run_command{command:<|\"|>mkdir frontend<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:run_command{value:<|\"|>$ mkdir frontend\n[exit 0]\n<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|channel>thought\nFrontend `package.json`.\n\n\n<channel|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"frontend\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"vite\",\n    \"build\": \"vite build\",\n    \"preview\": \"vite preview\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"vite\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>frontend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 236 chars to frontend/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|channel>thought\n","generated":""}
